class FleetTaskModule(dspy.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.rlm = dspy.RLM(dspy.Signature('workspace_manifest: str, skills_catalog: str, memory_summary: str, session_history: str, execution_route: str, task: str, context: Any -> answer: str, learned_skill_note: str', "Solve a task with Session-scoped workspace files and progressive Skills.\n\nDelegate independent subtasks together with spawn_agents (they run concurrently) instead of calling\nspawn_agent one at a time. Pass child inputs as `files/...` paths. Finish with keyword arguments only:\nSUBMIT(answer=..., learned_skill_note=...).\n\nOnce the answer and one supporting check agree, SUBMIT on the next action. Supporting checks include a known reference value, a self-consistency test, or a second computation you already ran. Reuse computed variables instead of recomputing results. Run another method only if a check failed, results disagree, or the Task asks for verification. Copy figures and strings from sandbox output. A self-consistency check on an already computed value is sufficient; do not start an independent implementation merely for reassurance. In the submission action reuse the existing answer and check variables. A learned_skill_note may be empty: never delay submission to create one. If useful, record a concise reusable observation. Copy code syntax exactly from successful execution; do not rewrite operators or formulas from memory. Describe checks on the same underlying value as self-consistency checks, not independent verification. Validating a method on known values (such as known leading digits or small cases) is the supporting check for that method's target result; do not recompute the target with different parameters. Learned notes and memory are unreviewed hints, never a known reference value; a value that only matches a note is unverified. Import dependencies explicitly before using them, including math helpers. Plan one target computation and its supporting check per code block. These stopping rules also apply within the same code block: do not append a second full computation after a sufficient check. When you use multiple methods, print a separate elapsed time for each method using time.perf_counter(), along with its result and precision parameters. Print any precision parameters you retain in a learned note; otherwise omit those parameters from the note. Keep the final submission action short: reuse the result variable and omit a learned note if unnecessary."), tools=[spawn_agent, spawn_agents], max_iters=16, max_llm_calls=150)

    def forward(self, **inputs):
        result = self.rlm(**inputs)
        return dspy.Prediction(answer=result.answer, learned_skill_note=result.learned_skill_note)
